+- **Dedup 按文件读取。** 同一个文件的多个线程：读取一次，一起判断。
 
-## Cross-item reasoning (when judging more than one item)
+## 跨项目推理（当判断多个项目时）
 
-You hold every thread at once -- use that:
+你可以同时持有每个线程——使用它：
 
-- **Cluster by root assumption.** If one source (often a bot) makes the same kind of claim across several threads and you find it doesn't hold in one place, scrutinize the siblings: a systematically-wrong premise produces a cluster of plausible-but-wrong findings. This is the single biggest advantage of judging centrally instead of per-isolated-agent.
-- **Converging requests are a strong fix signal.** The same change asked for by multiple independent reviewers rarely warrants a divert.
-- **A validated finding can span sites this PR itself introduced (fix the class, not one instance).** This is the mirror image of the wrong-reviewer cluster: a reviewer usually flags one occurrence, not all. When you accept a finding, check whether this change also introduced or touched *other* sites governed by the **same invariant** that admit the **same fix with no site-specific judgment**. When those sites are concretely identifiable and their treatment is unambiguous, fold them into **one** class fix (see full-mode Step 3) so the un-flagged twins don't come back later. Keep the class narrow: only behavior **this PR changed**, not every pre-existing occurrence in a touched file; exclude any site whose invariant or correct treatment differs (docs vs. code vs. fixtures, compatibility or intentionally-preserved paths). If equivalence or treatment is itself a judgment call, keep them separate items — a false "class complete" is worse than one more round.
-- **A finding on code an earlier review fix added asks whether that fix was the right layer.** Check whether the finding's lines came from a review-fix commit earlier on this branch (`git log` or `git blame` on those lines). If they did, the earlier fix and this finding share a premise: that the failure needed more code. Judge the finding against "Something already bounds the failure" under Diverts, including when it reports that the earlier fix does not work: the question is whether this finding's failure is already bounded, not whether the earlier fix achieved its aim. When it is bounded, do not add another layer; the reply names the earlier fix. When it is not, fix it as its own item; the usual case is behavior the earlier fix added that does more than the operator was shown, such as a filter the dry run honors and the real run ignores. The line's history raises the question; it does not answer it.
